\section{Put the Use at the Correct Program Point}

A value is live at a program point if it may be needed along a path starting there before the value is redefined. For SSA, distinguish a static definition from its dynamic execution: traversing a loop can execute the same defining instruction again. A use made by a $\phi$ operand is on its incoming edge; the $\phi$ result is defined on entry to the destination. Treating the input operands as ordinary uses in the join block can make a value appear to be live on other edges, even edges on which it is not defined.

A full backward solver makes the convention explicit. Let $I(S)$ denote liveness just after the simultaneous merges in $S$, and let $D_\phi(S)$ be the result names of those merges. Let $U_\phi(e)$ contain the value operands read on edge $e=B\to S$. The set transferred to the end of $B$ for that edge is
\[
  L(e) = \bigl(I(S)\setminus D_\phi(S)\bigr)\cup U_\phi(e).
\]
Union the outgoing sets at $B$, then scan its ordinary instructions backward, killing definitions and generating uses. This version counts operands of all remaining merges, even dead ones. Dead code elimination can remove a useless merge before liveness is computed. Keep instruction read and write points distinct when an operation may overwrite a register containing a last-use operand.

\section{A Diamond with Two Registers}

\begin{verbatim}
entry: if c then left else right
left:  a = input(); goto join
right: b = input(); goto join
join:  x = phi(left: a, right: b)
       return add(x, t)
\end{verbatim}

Here \texttt{t} is defined before the branch, the calls do not use it, and we are counting live SSA values, not call-clobber constraints. At the join just after the merge, the live names are \texttt{x} and \texttt{t}. Just before the copies implied by the left edge they are \texttt{a} and \texttt{t}, and on the right they are \texttt{b} and \texttt{t}. Nothing here makes \texttt{a} and \texttt{b} live together. In an ideal two-register target the three names \texttt{a}, \texttt{b}, and \texttt{x} can occupy the same register, and the merge needs no moves. As a regression, ask separately for the live sets on both edges: a solver reporting both incoming operands at either edge is not implementing the specified use positions.

\section{Queries and Pressure Are Different Products}

Boissinot et al.'s \emph{Fast Liveness Checking for SSA-Form Programs} separates CFG-dependent preprocessing from queries using a value's current use-def information. Dominance and precomputed reachability information help decide whether a point can reach a use without crossing a new execution of the definition. This is useful when a transformation makes many local changes and repeatedly asks about individual values. The CFG preprocessing can survive changes to definitions and uses with an unchanged graph; a cached answer to the same value query cannot be assumed to survive them. To estimate pressure at every instruction, a bulk liveness computation may still be more appropriate.

For strict SSA under an idealized uniform register model, live ranges can be viewed as connected subtrees of a dominator tree refined to program points. Their intersection graph is chordal; its maximum clique size equals the largest simultaneous live set, and it can be colored with that many registers. Bouchez, Darte, Guillon, and Rastello's \emph{Register Allocation and Spill Complexity under SSA} contrasts this spill-free assignment result with the difficulty of choosing what to spill. The theorem does not choose the cheapest reload points, honor fixed calling-convention registers, or account for register classes, subregister overlap, and tied operands.

