\section{Compare Pressure at Read and Write Points}

A block's live-in and live-out sets can both be small even when
the allocator has too few registers halfway through the block.
For a local pressure audit, take a straight-line group of ordinary
instructions and a live-out set already computed by the CFG solver.
Count one unit per live value in one register class. The toy target
reads every source before writing any destination; it has no fixed
registers, early clobbers, or tied operands. Record live sets on both
sides of each instruction rather than inferring simultaneous demand
from the union of those sets.

\begin{sudocode}
\Require Instructions $I$, live-out names $L$
\Let{P}{|L|}
\ForAll{$i\in I$ in reverse order}
  \To{This set describes values surviving the write.}
  \State Record $L$ after $i$
  \Let{L}{(L\setminus\operatorname{defs}(i))\cup\operatorname{uses}(i)}
  \To{Last-use operands are still needed at the read.}
  \State Record $L$ before $i$
  \Let{P}{\max(P,|L|)}
\EndFor
\State \Return the recorded sets and peak $P$
\end{sudocode}

Let $a=4$ and $b=7$ already be available. Compute
$x=a+2$, then $y=b+3$, then return $x+y$ in mathematical
integer arithmetic. The return reads $\{x,y\}$. Just before
the second addition the set is $\{x,b\}$; just before the
first it is $\{a,b\}$. Every set has size at most two, and
the result is $16$. Counting both $b$ and its replacement
$y$ alongside $x$ would report a spurious peak of three.
Now add an observation of $b$ after defining $y$. The set
just after that definition really is $\{x,y,b\}$, so two
registers alone cannot hold all three values there.

Figure~\ref{fig:visual-206} draws the live ranges on either side of the second addition, with and without the later read of \(b\).

