At each reverse step the invariant is that $L$ describes
exactly the current boundary under the chosen liveness
convention. Maintaining the count incrementally avoids
copying whole sets if only the peak is needed. This gives
value pressure, not a complete machine feasibility test:
a dead result may still need a scratch destination, and an
early-clobber instruction can prevent source reuse even
when the abstract read and write sets each fit. Report such
constraints separately rather than adjusting the liveness
equation to fit one opcode.

\section{Measure What a Transformation Changes}

Moving a computation earlier can lower dynamic instruction count but leave its result live across a call or across a point already at pressure capacity. Track the peak before and after the move in each relevant register class, then estimate spill cost on the affected paths, rather than equating the total number of SSA names with register demand. In a managed VM, a safepoint can also demand live locations for references or values needed to reconstruct an interpreter frame. The dossier's Chapter~4, \emph{Deoptimization as an IR Operation}, gives a reason to model those reconstruction operands as uses, even if no ordinary instruction reads them. Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-208} shows why concrete location constraints are an additional layer, not a replacement for liveness.
